{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/specs/2026-11-17-pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place.md .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md; sed -n '1,165p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_policy_baseline.go; sed -n '540,610p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go; rg -n 'viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr|reviewProcessGroupAvailable|resolveReviewerContainment|verifyReviewProcessGroup' pose-mcp/internal/pose\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/specs/2026-11-17-pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place.md .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md; sed -n '1,165p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_policy_baseline.go; sed -n '540,610p' pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go; rg -n 'viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr|reviewProcessGroupAvailable|resolveReviewerContainment|verifyReviewProcessGroup' pose-mcp/internal/pose\"","aggregated_output":"---\nslug: pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-17\ncompleted_at:        # stamped on the transition to status: done\nsupersedes:          # slug of the superseded spec (when applicable)\ndepends_on: pose-federated-source-proof-is-computed-once-per-operation\nremediates:          # optional explicit links: spec:slug@defect-fix, finding:rva-<16hex>/F1@revert (never inferred from depends_on)\npriority:            # integer >= 0 (lower = higher priority); ordering preference, not a blocker\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: refactor\nsurface:             # optional: minimal | standard | full (default) — minimal drops Tasks and derivable report sections; `pose specs facts <slug>` derives them\ndelivers: capability:review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nTirar duas despesas fixas de cada verificação de bundle: a cópia profunda do grafo de integridade de entrega que o guard de contrato do plano de revisão pedia a cada chamada, e a varredura de todo o `/proc` que a sonda de grupo de processos fazia a cada verificação.\n\n### Business value\nÉ o follow-up F1 de `pose-federated-source-proof-is-computed-once-per-operation`. No perfil da primeira chamada `pose_closeout_state` sobre o harne8, `resolveReviewGovernanceGuard` pedia `GetDeliveryIntegrity`, que devolve uma cópia profunda do grafo em cache (cerca de 5,5 s de CPU em `copyDeliveryIntegrityGraph`) só para ler os change sets do escopo; e `reviewProcessGroupAvailable` lia todos os `/proc/<pid>/stat` da máquina a cada verificação para saber se o sistema permite observar o fim de um grupo de processos (cerca de 4,3 s), uma propriedade que não muda entre duas verificações de um fechamento.\n\n### Constraints\n- O guard continua dando os mesmos caminhos e a mesma revisão base, e nunca altera nem guarda o grafo em cache.\n- Uma sonda que falhou nunca é guardada; um sucesso vale por um minuto.\n\n### Non-goals\n- Mudar o grafo, o cache dele ou o que a sonda verifica.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: The review plan's governance guard shall read the delivery-integrity graph through the cache's in-place view, deriving its paths and base revision inside the view and keeping nothing the graph reaches.\n- R2: The guard shall give the same paths and base revision as a deep copy of the graph gives, and the retained graph shall be unchanged after it.\n- R3: The process-group availability probe shall keep a success for one minute within the process and shall never keep a failure.\n\n### Non-functional\n- No harne8, a primeira chamada de fechamento cai cerca de 10% (média de 63 s para 56,5 s em rodadas alternadas), com o mesmo veredito.\n\n### Security\n- O guard só lê o grafo; a sonda guarda apenas o instante de um sucesso.\n\n### Compatibility\n- Sem mudança de contrato, saída ou formato.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n- `pose/delivery_integrity.go`: `Store.viewDeliveryIntegrity`, sobre `viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr`.\n- `pose/review_policy_baseline.go`: o guard deriva caminhos e revisão dentro da view.\n- `pose/review_dispatch_unix.go`: a sonda guarda um sucesso por `processGroupProbeTTL`.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_in_place_test.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_policy_baseline.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispatch_unix.go\n- modified: .pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n- capability:review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place module:pose-mcp profile:composed-capability entrypoint:pose-mcp/cmd/pose/main.go\n\nComposto em produção por todo plano de revisão (o guard) e por toda verificação que declara a contenção do revisor (a sonda). Check permanente `review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place-integration`, evidenceClass `integration`, required: `go test ./internal/pose -run 'TestReviewGuardReadsTheIntegrityGraphInPlace|TestProcessGroupProbeKeepsASuccessForItsTTL|TestABMProtectedBaseline' -count=1`.\n\n### API/contract changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Data/storage changes\n- Nenhuma.\n\n### Technical risks\n- Um sistema que perca o acesso a `/proc` no meio de um processo ainda declara a capacidade por até um minuto; a capacidade declarada é a de observar o término, e a execução de um revisor continua verificando o grupo de verdade.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Guard pela view do cache (R1, R2).\n- [x] Sonda com sucesso guardado (R3).\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Testes de equivalência, de grafo intacto e da sonda, com prova de reprovação; medição no harne8.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\n- Date: 2026-11-17\n- Context: o follow-up F1 de `pose-federated-source-proof-is-computed-once-per-operation` mediu os custos fixos de cada prova distinta.\n- Options considered: (a) guard pela view sem cópia e sonda com sucesso guardado; (b) memo por operação do grafo copiado; (c) só a sonda.\n- Decision: (a).\n- Basis: R1, R2, R3\n- Rationale: (b) ainda copiaria o grafo uma vez por produtor; a view já existe no cache para leitores; (c) sozinha mal aparece na medição.\n- Consequences: o guard não pode guardar nada do grafo além das strings que deriva.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nTeste de equivalência do guard contra uma cópia do grafo, no fixture que muda o contrato de revisão; teste do grafo retido intacto; teste da sonda com a verificação substituída; os testes `TestABMProtectedBaseline*` do guard; medição no harne8.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/pose ./internal/cli ./internal/mcpserver ./internal/scaffold -count=1`\n- Scope: pose-mcp\n- Expected: verde\n\n### Execution log\n- Date: 2026-11-17\n- Environment: Linux, Go 1.26, Git 2.56; clone do harne8 em cc7780b1 com o pose-dist em 7e46b1e e commit-graph com filtros; `closeout-check spec:harne8-abm-governed-execution`, alternando o binário anterior e o novo.\n- Notes: 64 s e 62 s com o anterior, 60 s e 53 s com o novo, terminais. O teste do grafo intacto reprova com um guard que escreve no grafo; o da sonda reprova sem o sucesso guardado.\n\n### Results summary\n- Successes: cerca de 10% a menos na primeira chamada, mesmo veredito.\n- Failures: nenhuma.\n- Warnings: a medição teve outra carga na máquina; os números são médias de duas rodadas.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestReviewGuardReadsTheIntegrityGraphInPlace check:review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place-integration\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestReviewGuardReadsTheIntegrityGraphInPlace\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestProcessGroupProbeKeepsASuccessForItsTTL\n\n### Known gaps\n- Nenhuma.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n\nNão concluída.\n\n### Residual risks\n\n### Follow-ups\n\n### Review\n\nA primeira tentativa da rodada 1 não chegou ao revisor: o `pose validate --strict` reprovou em `release-test-compile-windows` (`GOOS=windows go vet ./internal/pose`), porque o teste da sonda usava `processGroupVerify` e `processGroupProbe`, que só existem em `review_dispatch_unix.go`. O teste da sonda foi para `review_guard_probe_unix_test.go`, sob a mesma restrição `//go:build !windows`.\n\n# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Documentation Style\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for process documentation, rules, specs, workflows, and operational instructions.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Write instructions in the imperative mood and begin them with action verbs.\n- Keep bullets short and limited to one idea.\n- Avoid duplicating sections with the same purpose across files.\n- Link to the single source of truth instead of copying its content.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- State each instruction's scope explicitly to reduce ambiguity.\n\n## Examples: good and bad\n\n### Redundancy\n\n- **Good:** \"Update review criteria in `.pose/workflows/review.md` and reference that workflow from the root AGENTS file.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Repeat review criteria in AGENTS, the workflow, and every related spec.\"\n\n### Ambiguous reference\n\n- **Good:** \"Run the lint check described by the review workflow.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Run that standard validation before pushing.\"\n\n## Quick editorial checklist\n\n- Language is imperative and direct.\n- Bullets are short and non-overlapping.\n- Files do not duplicate sections.\n- `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` are used consistently.\n- References point to explicit files or paths.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\npackage pose\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\n// A diff that changes the review contract cannot be the authority that approves\n// itself. When the reviewed scope touches the review policy or a review profile,\n// the obligations are resolved a second time from the contract as it stood at the\n// change set's resolved base, and anything the diff weakened is restored for the\n// duration of this review. Adopting a weaker contract stays possible; approving\n// it under the weaker contract does not.\n//\n// Those two directories are the contract: the policy and the profiles carry their\n// own schema versions, so there is no separate schema path to guard. Rule bodies\n// under .pose/rules are deliberately outside this gate — a criterion names a rule,\n// and it is the criterion contract that is compared here.\n//\n// The restoration is not a second policy engine: it loads the same files through\n// the same parsers and composes them through the same selector and composer, at\n// a different revision. What it adds is direction — obligations may be restored\n// upward, never downward.\nconst (\n\treviewGovernancePolicyPrefix  = \".pose/policy/\"\n\treviewGovernanceProfilePrefix = \".pose/review-profiles/\"\n\n\t// A policy or profile is a small JSON document. The bound exists so a\n\t// crafted blob at the base revision cannot be read without limit.\n\treviewBaselineMaxBytes = 1 << 20\n)\n\n// ReviewPlanPolicyBaseline reports whether this plan was resolved under a\n// protected contract and what that protection changed.\ntype ReviewPlanPolicyBaseline struct {\n\tProtected bool `json:\"protected\"`\n\t// Reason is filled when the scope changes the contract but protection could\n\t// not be established. It is stated rather than silently skipped: an\n\t// unprotected governance review is the failure this field exists to expose.\n\tReason   string   `json:\"reason,omitempty\"`\n\tRevision string   `json:\"revision,omitempty\"`\n\tPaths    []string `json:\"paths,omitempty\"`\n\t// Weakened lists contract changes observed in the diff. Restored lists the\n\t// obligations this plan put back for the review of that diff.\n\tWeakened []string `json:\"weakened,omitempty\"`\n\tRestored []string `json:\"restored,omitempty\"`\n}\n\nfunc reviewGovernanceContractPath(path string) bool {\n\tclean := strings.TrimPrefix(filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path)), \"./\")\n\treturn strings.HasPrefix(clean, reviewGovernancePolicyPrefix) ||\n\t\tstrings.HasPrefix(clean, reviewGovernanceProfilePrefix)\n}\n\n// reviewGovernanceGuard is what the plan knows before it trusts the working\n// tree's policy: which contract paths this scope changes, and the revision whose\n// contract governed before it did.\ntype reviewGovernanceGuard struct {\n\tPaths    []string\n\tRevision string\n\tReason   string\n\t// Unreadable records that the working tree's own contract could not be read\n\t// for this scope, so the protected one governed in its place.\n\tUnreadable string\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) resolveReviewGovernanceGuard(scope ScopeRef) reviewGovernanceGuard {\n\tguard := reviewGovernanceGuard{}\n\tspecs, err := s.reviewScopeSpecs(scope)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn guard\n\t}\n\twanted := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor _, spec := range specs {\n\t\twanted[spec.Slug] = true\n\t\tclaims, found, claimErr := ParseArtifactClaims(spec, ArtifactPolicy{})\n\t\tif claimErr != nil || !found {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfor _, claim := range claims {\n\t\t\tfor _, path := range []string{claim.Path, claim.OldPath, claim.NewPath} {\n\t\t\t\tif path != \"\" && reviewGovernanceContractPath(path) {\n\t\t\t\t\tguard.Paths = append(guard.Paths, filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path)))\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// The guard only reads the graph: it reads the retained one in place and\n\t// keeps nothing it reaches, only the strings it derives.\n\terr = s.viewDeliveryIntegrity(func(graph DeliveryIntegrityGraph) {\n\t\tsets := []*ChangeSet{}\n\t\tfor i := range graph.ChangeSets {\n\t\t\tif wanted[graph.ChangeSets[i].Spec] {\n\t\t\t\tsets = append(sets, &graph.ChangeSets[i])\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tsort.Slice(sets, func(i, j int) bool { return sets[i].ID < sets[j].ID })\n\t\tfor _, set := range sets {\n\t\t\tfor _, observed := range set.Paths {\n\t\t\t\tfor _, path := range []string{observed.Path, observed.OldPath, observed.NewPath} {\n\t\t\t\t\tif path != \"\" && reviewGovernanceContractPath(path) {\n\t\t\t\t\t\tguard.Paths = append(guard.Paths, filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path)))\n\t\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t// Mirror the review subject: the first attributed set, ordered by id,\n\t\t\t// supplies the base. A scope with several sets is reviewed against the\n\t\t\t// contract that governed before any of them landed.\n\t\t\tif guard.Revision == \"\" {\n\t\t\t\tguard.Revision = set.ResolvedBase\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tif len(guard.Paths) > 0 {\n\t\t\tguard.Reason = \"delivery-integrity index is unavailable, so no base revision is attributable; run `pose index`\"\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn guard\n\t}\n\tguard.Paths = uniqueSorted(guard.Paths)\n\tif len(guard.Paths) > 0 && guard.Revision == \"\" {\n\t\tguard.Reason = \"no attributed change set resolves a base revision for the contract change\"\n\t}\n\treturn guard\n}\n\n// reviewPolicyBaseline is the contract as it stood at a revision.\ntype reviewPolicyBaseline struct {\n\tRevision string\n\tPolicy   ReviewPolicy\n\tBase     ReviewProfile\n\tOverlays []ReviewProfile\n\tResolved bool\n}\n\n// reviewBaselineRevision refuses anything that is not an immutable object name\n// before it reaches a Git argument. The revision arrives from an index file in\n// the repository, so it is input: a value beginning with `-` would be read by\n// Git as an option rather than a revision, and a symbolic name would make the\n// protected contract depend on where a ref points today. The structural detector\n// already holds its subject to this shape; the baseline had not.\nfunc reviewBaselineRevision(revision string) error {\n\tif !designDeltaRevisionRE.MatchString(revision) {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: %q is not an immutable Git revision\", revision)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) parseReviewPolicyAt(revision string) (ReviewPolicy, error) {\n\tif err := reviewBaselineRevision(revision); err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewPolicy{}, err\n\t}\n\traw, err := gitShowBounded(s.Root, revision, \".pose/policy/review.json\", reviewBaselineMaxBytes)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewPolicy{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: reading review policy at %s: %w\", revision, err)\n\t}\n\treturn s.parseReviewPolicy(raw)\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) parseReviewProfileAt(revision, ref string) (ReviewProfile, error) {\n\tif err := reviewBaselineRevision(revision); err != nil {\n\t\treturn ReviewProfile{}, err\n\t}\n// BuildDeliveryIntegrityWithReleases reads and writes the filesystem directly; BuildDeliveryIntegrityWithReleasesIn takes the\n// operation's files.\nfunc BuildDeliveryIntegrityWithReleases(specs []Spec, claims []ArtifactClaim, changeSets []ChangeSet, tracked []string, policy ArtifactPolicy, archived []ArchivedFragment) DeliveryIntegrityGraph {\n\treturn BuildDeliveryIntegrityWithReleasesIn(NativeFiles(), specs, claims, changeSets, tracked, policy, archived)\n}\n\n// viewDeliveryIntegrity hands fn the index's retained graph without copying\n// it, for callers that only read it: fn must neither modify nor keep the graph\n// or anything it reaches (spec pose-review-guard-reads-the-integrity-graph-in-place).\nfunc (s Store) viewDeliveryIntegrity(fn func(DeliveryIntegrityGraph)) error {\n\traw, _, err := s.ReadIndex(\"delivery-integrity.json\")\n\tif errors.Is(err, ErrIndexGenerationUnavailable) {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pose: delivery-integrity index not found; run `pose index`\")\n\t}\n\tif err := viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(s.Root, raw, fn); err != nil {\n\t\treturn deliveryIndexError(err)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\nfunc (s Store) GetDeliveryIntegrity(path string) (DeliveryIntegrityGraph, error) {\n\traw, _, err := s.ReadIndex(\"delivery-integrity.json\")\n\tif errors.Is(err, ErrIndexGenerationUnavailable) {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, err\n\t}\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: delivery-integrity index not found; run `pose index`\")\n\t}\n\tgraph, perr := parseDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(s.Root, raw)\n\tif perr != nil {\n\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, deliveryIndexError(perr)\n\t}\n\tif path != \"\" {\n\t\tif err := ValidateArtifactPathIn(s.fsys(), s.Root, path, false); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn DeliveryIntegrityGraph{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\twanted := filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path))\n\t\tgraph.Reverse = map[string][]string{wanted: graph.Reverse[wanted]}\n\t\t// Allocates rather than filtering through the backing array: the graph\n\t\t// may come from the parse cache, and writing through it would narrow\n\t\t// every later caller's findings to this one path.\n\t\tfiltered := []DeliveryIntegrityFinding{}\n\t\tfor _, finding := range graph.Findings {\n\t\t\tif finding.Path == wanted {\n\t\t\t\tfiltered = append(filtered, finding)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tgraph.Findings = filtered\n\t}\n\treturn graph, nil\n}\n\nvar datePrefixRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}-`)\n\nfunc normalizeSpecPath(p string) string {\n\tp = filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(p))\n\tif !strings.HasPrefix(p, \".pose/specs/\") {\n\t\treturn p\n\t}\n\trel := strings.TrimPrefix(p, \".pose/specs/\")\n\tif strings.HasSuffix(rel, \"/spec.md\") {\n\t\tslug := strings.TrimSuffix(rel, \"/spec.md\")\n\t\tslug = datePrefixRE.ReplaceAllString(slug, \"\")\n\t\treturn \".pose/specs/\" + slug\n\t}\n\tif strings.HasSuffix(rel, \".md\") && !strings.Contains(rel, \"/\") {\n\t\tslug := strings.TrimSuffix(rel, \".md\")\n\t\tslug = datePrefixRE.ReplaceAllString(slug, \"\")\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:34:\tif reviewProcessGroupAvailable() == nil || reviewProcessGroupAvailable() == nil || probes != 2 {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:39:\t\tif err := reviewProcessGroupAvailable(); err != nil {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/review_guard_probe_unix_test.go:49:\treviewProcessGroupAvailable()\npose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity_cache.go:79:\treturn viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(owner, raw, fn) == nil\npose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity_cache.go:82:func viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(owner string, raw []byte, fn func(DeliveryIntegrityGraph)) error {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity_cache.go:105:\tif err := viewDeliveryIntegrityGraphErr(s.Root, raw, fn); err != nil {\npose